# From SŪTRADHĀRA — the seed-ownership question is no longer an argument. It is a measurement.

Adding to my earlier escalation, because M0-T27 changed the evidence available for that ruling.

## It built a post-reseed projection

Rather than reasoning about what a re-seed would do, it **modelled it**: a CTE shadowing
`asset_registry` with the values `asset_registry_seed.ts` would write, then re-ran every passing
rule against that shadow. Durability stopped being an argument and became a number.

What it found:

- **Criterion 11 — one of M0's own v4.1 acceptance criteria — has a NON-DURABLE PASS.** One cell
  causes it: `mi_jivanaghatana` is live `scope=per_chart` / `domain=chart`, and the seed declares
  `scope:'global'`. `scope` is seed-overwritten; `domain` is not. So a re-seed moves **one half of
  the pair** and leaves the row internally inconsistent — a `domain` that no longer matches the
  `scope` it was derived from.
- **M0-T21's `asset_kind`/`asset_type` repair is reverted on all 8 divergent rows.** Confirms my
  reading of the upsert, by projection rather than by inspection.
- **M0-T20's `has_substeps` repair is durable** — that column is in neither the seed's INSERT nor
  its DO UPDATE list. It verified this rather than inheriting my claim.

It reported the non-durable criterion **as PASS with the durability stated on the row**, rather
than downgrading it — the honest reading, since the criterion is genuinely satisfied today and
the risk is about tomorrow.

## And it found another detector that could not fail

**Criterion 10's own detector could not fail on the word "blocking."** It computed
`PASS if (guard file exists) AND (a workflow names it)` — run the *unmodified* generator against
today's tree and it reports **PASS**, while both jobs are `continue-on-error: true`, neither guard
nor workflow is on `main`, and `gh` reports zero runs. It now parses `continue-on-error` per job
and per step and checks `origin/main`. Result: **FAIL**, correctly. It did not flip the switch.

That is the fifth constant-wearing-a-detector's-clothes found today, and the third found *inside
the campaign's own instruments* rather than in the codebase they audit.

It also ran a falsifiability probe over every rule now reading zero — each one mutated
deliberately, **all ten fired**. So no PASS in this scorecard comes from a query that could not
have returned non-zero.

## The tally moved in both directions, and it graded against itself

| | first reading | now |
|---|--:|--:|
| PASS | 0 | **1** |
| FAIL | 6 | **7** |
| NOT-MEASURABLE | 3 | 3 |
| BLOCKED | 3 | **1** |

FAIL went **up**, and that is progress: criterion 7 moved BLOCKED → FAIL because migration 590
gave it a detector and **the detector immediately returned 1**. Not a new defect — a newly visible
one. Its offender is `ka_gochara_sweep`, so it is flagged and not resolved.

The KĀRAKA noted that grading it FAIL rather than BLOCKED is genuinely arguable, and said it
**chose the reading that does not flatter the tally**. I would rather it kept doing that.

## What I have dispatched, and what I am still holding

**M0-T29** extends the projection to **all** the seed's overwritten columns. M0-T27 modelled 4 of
23 and declared itself a lower bound, which is exactly why its result can be trusted — but a
ruling on seed-vs-DB ownership should rest on the full picture, not on four columns.

**Phase 0.5a stays held.** `layer_index` and `layer_name` are both in the overwritten set, so
dispatching that repair now would spend a KĀRAKA writing 21 rows the next seed run reverts, and
would put a green in the scorecard that production would later contradict. That is the campaign
manufacturing its own stale signal, which I am not willing to do while the question is open in
front of you.

One thing that keeps this from being urgent in wall-clock terms: the seeder still cannot complete
a run at all, because of the `bg_cohort` formula abort. As with `DRY_RUN`, an unrelated defect is
holding the line — and as with `DRY_RUN`, it is one line from being fixed by someone who knows
nothing about any of this.

— SŪTRADHĀRA
